/** @page avrogettingstarted Getting Started with the avro Module

    @tableofcontents

    @section avrogettingstartedintro Overview

    Everything in the module starts with an @ref Qore::Avro::AvroSchema "AvroSchema" object. It is
    parsed once, is immutable afterwards, and is what encodes and decodes data.

    @section avrogettingstartedparse Parsing a Schema

    A schema is a JSON document. Pass it as text:

    @code{.py}
%requires avro

AvroSchema schema('{
    "type": "record",
    "name": "com.example.Event",
    "fields": [
        {"name": "id", "type": "string"},
        {"name": "count", "type": "long"},
        {"name": "note", "type": ["null", "string"], "default": null}
    ]
}');
    @endcode

    or as %Qore data, when it has already been parsed or is being generated programmatically:

    @code{.py}
AvroSchema schema({
    "type": "record",
    "name": "com.example.Event",
    "fields": (
        {"name": "id", "type": "string"},
        {"name": "count", "type": "long"},
        {"name": "note", "type": ("null", "string"), "default": NOTHING},
    ),
});
    @endcode

    Any Avro schema is accepted, not only records — a bare primitive name, an array, a map, or a
    top-level union all work.

    @section avrogettingstartedcodec Encoding and Decoding a Bare Datum

    @code{.py}
binary data = schema.encode({"id": "abc", "count": 7, "note": NOTHING});
hash<auto> event = schema.decode(data);
printf("%s: %d\n", event.id, event.count);
    @endcode

    A bare datum carries no length, so @ref Qore::Avro::AvroSchema::decode() "decode()" reads one
    value and ignores anything after it. If the input must be validated as a whole, use a
    container file.

    @section avrogettingstartedreuse Reusing a Schema

    Parsing a schema costs far more than encoding or decoding one datum with it, and an
    %AvroSchema is immutable and thread-safe once constructed. Applications that decode a stream
    of data against schemas fetched by ID should cache the parsed objects:

    @code{.py}
hash<string, AvroSchema> cache;

AvroSchema getSchema(string schema_id) {
    if (!cache{schema_id}) {
        cache{schema_id} = new AvroSchema(fetchSchemaJson(schema_id));
    }
    return cache{schema_id};
}

hash<auto> decodeEvent(string schema_id, binary payload) {
    return getSchema(schema_id).decode(payload);
}
    @endcode

    When the transport gives no stable ID, key the cache on the schema's own identity instead:
    @ref Qore::Avro::AvroSchema::getFingerprint() "getFingerprint()" returns the CRC-64-AVRO
    fingerprint of the @ref Qore::Avro::AvroSchema::getCanonicalForm() "Parsing Canonical Form",
    so two schemas that differ only in documentation, aliases, default values or whitespace share
    one entry.

    @section avrogettingstartedcontainer Reading and Writing Container Files

    A container file needs no external schema — it carries its own:

    @code{.py}
{
    AvroFileWriter writer("events.avro", schema, {"codec": "deflate"});
    writer.write({"id": "a", "count": 1});
    writer.write({"id": "b", "count": 2});
}

AvroFileReader reader("events.avro");
map printf("%y\n", $1), reader;
    @endcode

    @ref Qore::Avro::AvroFileWriter "AvroFileWriter" buffers objects and writes them in blocks;
    the last block is written by
    @ref Qore::Avro::AvroFileWriter::close() "close()" or by destroying the object, which is why
    the writer above is scoped.

    @section avrogettingstartednext Next Steps

    - @ref avrocookbook — a worked example for every type, logical type and framing
    - @ref avroschemaguide — the type mapping, union branch selection and schema resolution rules
*/
